the phrase.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:15.700
I don't know as in, I don't know the answer
to that.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.700
Oh, Hey David.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.300
Uh, if this gets through then, uh, cool.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.540
Uh, so I just wanted to see what your thoughts
were about this sort of concept.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.650
Um, I feel as though many political figures
as well as just people in general are sort

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.080
of scared as they think.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.129
Like, I don't know, or I need to look more
into that.

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:39.379
And that sort of thing, uh, in the landscape
of like seemingly endless political facts.

00:00:39.379 --> 00:00:44.890
Personally, I would love the sort of authenticity,
uh, but I think many people are scared of

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.500
like a Gary Johnson, Aleppo kind of situation.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.320
Um, so anyway, let me know what your thoughts
were about that.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.399
Yeah, I've been talking about this for awhile.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:59.219
I would much rather hear from people, I don't
know enough about that yet to have an informed

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:05.600
opinion and uh, I, I simply, I'm not in a
position to take a strong, uh, position one

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.050
way or the other on that right now.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:14.400
The problem is that while if you don't know
something you, you should, you should be comfortable

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.570
saying that you are under informed about that.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:23.750
You also might be in a position where you
should know about that thing.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.750
Right?

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:31.240
So in other words, when Herman Cain was running
in 2008 or 2012, I think it was, and on so

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.371
many issues, he said, I don't know, but I'm
going to put in a team and they're going to

00:01:35.371 --> 00:01:36.600
figure it all out.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.250
At a certain point you've got to know something
about important issues.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:45.320
If you are trying to say, I should be elected
to do something or I should be the CEO or

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.280
whatever the case may be, there is no problem
in principle with recognizing where you lack

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.010
information or you lack knowledge and simply
saying that you are going to get more informed

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.530
as a societal principle.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:56.770
That is a good thing.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:03.060
That doesn't mean that being uninformed in
every situation is innocent or not something

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.450
that we should be concerned about.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.450
So I think that's a difference.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.560
When Gary Johnson said, what's Aleppo?

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:13.480
Um, the issue was not necessarily that he
didn't have a fully fledged out policy on

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.360
Aleppo and troop movements and foreign policy
issues around that.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.940
That's one thing.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.640
He seemed to have no idea what Aleppo is.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.000
The fact that Aleppo is a sear is a city.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Right?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.170
So that that was the problem.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.450
And while as a society, we should encourage
recognizing we have information shortfalls,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.390
understanding where we lack a new and nuanced
enough understanding of an issue to take a

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:36.390
position.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.340
There are times where if you don't know, you
probably just shouldn't be in the position

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.910
you're arguing you should be in, and it's
important to keep those two things separate.

